{"duration_api_ms":18019,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"9a62597f-3602-4fc4-b449-32ee5a862e07","total_cost_usd":0.23959999999999998,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":35288,"cache_read_input_tokens":132800,"output_tokens":1828,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":375},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":35288},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":413,"cache_read_input_tokens":34933,"cache_creation_input_tokens":355,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":355,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":10,"outputTokens":1828,"cacheReadInputTokens":132800,"cacheCreationInputTokens":35288,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.23959999999999998,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":375,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":5,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"The recovery middleware now treats a panic with `http.ErrAbortHandler` the same way it treats a broken pipe. It logs one short line instead of a stack trace and doesn't write a 500. It records the error on the context and aborts the request. The Go standard library uses this panic on purpose to stop a handler (for example, its reverse proxy does), and its own HTTP server doesn't print a stack trace for it either.\n\n- **Fix:** `recovery.go` checks `errors.Is(err, http.ErrAbortHandler)`, so it also catches the error when it's wrapped.\n- **Test:** I added `TestPanicWithAbortHandler` to `recovery_test.go`. The panic and recovery tests pass.\n\n**Not committed:** the commit failed because this repo has no git name or email configured. I didn't set one for you. After you configure it, run `git commit -am \"fix(recover): suppress http.ErrAbortHandler in recover\"`.\n\n**Behaviour change:** the panic stops in this middleware and never reaches `net/http`. So the server no longer closes the connection itself, which is the abort `ErrAbortHandler` normally triggers. Broken pipes are already handled this way.","ttft_ms":1832,"type":"result","duration_ms":20570,"uuid":"96f9d23d-ad83-46e9-99fb-cbfce0e8153b","ttft_stream_ms":835,"time_to_request_ms":143,"first_content_frame_ms":835,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
